 "notes": [
  "Circuit: MIDI jack pin 5 -> 220 ohm -> Uno digital pin 1 (TX); pin 2 -> GND; pin 4 -> 220 ohm -> +5V.",
  "The validator rejected the connector-type MIDI jack (pin ports) when it was plugged into breadboard holes. I switched to the catalogue's `midi-jack` (MIDI Socket, 5-pin DIN). Its pins are leads that can go into breadboard holes. It is the same physical female DIN jack.",
  "In the picture the jack has short leads (yellow and red) at columns 7 and 9. I modelled jack pins 5 and 4 as inserted into holes 7b.g and 9b.g.",
  "The picture shows the black wire running to the jack's center pin (2). A wire end cannot join a bare jack pin, so I put pin 2 into breadboard hole 8b.j. The black jumper goes from hole 8b.i, on the same strip, to Uno GND.1 (the GND next to AREF). The electrical result is the same.",
  "The picture's breadboard is drawn with rows j-f at the top and e-a at the bottom. The catalogue names the bank 't' for rows a-e and 'b' for rows f-j. The resistors therefore bridge the centre gap between bank 'b' (rows f-j) and bank 't' (rows a-e) at columns 7 and 9.",
  "Each resistor is symmetric, so its leg assignment is arbitrary.",
  "On Leonardo or Due boards the sketch should use Serial1. The Uno wiring is shown here."
 ],
